The surest way to trust a protocol is to watch it run. pancake ships runnable
examples at three levels: short Zig chapters that peel the library one layer at a
time, small C programs that exercise the same library through its C ABI, and full
cross-language apps where a coordinator in one language talks to clients in four
others over real [OCapN](https://github.com/ocapn/ocapn). Every one compiles and
runs under the build, so the code on the page cannot drift from the library.

## The Zig chapters

They read top-down. Chapter 01 is the surface an application rides; chapter 02
widens it to the multi-connection `Host` tier; chapters 03–05 then descend one
layer at a time to the raw engine; chapter 06 returns to the actor layer for
pipelining and attenuation.

| # | Chapter | What it shows |
| --- | --- | --- |
| 01 | [connect & serve](https://codeberg.org/jimisaacs/pancake/src/branch/main/examples/zig/01_connect_and_serve.zig) | the application surface over a real TCP socket: listen, accept, publish by name, serve; connect, fetch, call, switch on the outcome |
| 02 | [introduce and redeem](https://codeberg.org/jimisaacs/pancake/src/branch/main/examples/zig/02_introduce_and_redeem.zig) | the capability pattern itself: an introduction settles as the introduced capability (the host redeems the gift inside the call), and the direct call leaves the introducer behind |
| 03 | [actor far-send](https://codeberg.org/jimisaacs/pancake/src/branch/main/examples/zig/03_actor_far_send.zig) | the `Vat` pattern beneath a call: publish an actor, far-send to it, settle the promise |
| 04 | [look up by name](https://codeberg.org/jimisaacs/pancake/src/branch/main/examples/zig/04_look_up_by_name.zig) | the bootstrap fetch by swiss number that sits under fetch-by-name |
| 05 | [call a remote method](https://codeberg.org/jimisaacs/pancake/src/branch/main/examples/zig/05_call_a_remote_method.zig) | the raw engine: pair two peers and shuttle deliver and resolve one `step` at a time |
| 06 | [pipeline and attenuate](https://codeberg.org/jimisaacs/pancake/src/branch/main/examples/zig/06_pipeline_and_attenuate.zig) | promise pipelining and attenuation together: dependent calls aimed at an unresolved answer, and a reply that forwards only the allow-listed verbs |

`zig build examples` builds and runs them all, and they also run under
`zig build check`. The
[example chapters](https://codeberg.org/jimisaacs/pancake/src/branch/main/examples/README.md)
carry the full source and a few lower-level mechanics besides.

## The C programs

Four C programs prove the same library from the other side of the C ABI, under
the same `zig build examples` step as the chapters.

| Program | What it shows |
| --- | --- |
| [echo_peer.c](https://codeberg.org/jimisaacs/pancake/src/branch/main/examples/c/echo_peer.c) | a full in-process echo conversation between two peers, the reference shape of a C host event loop over the sans-io surface |
| [frame_view.c](https://codeberg.org/jimisaacs/pancake/src/branch/main/examples/c/frame_view.c) | frame ownership over the C ABI: a borrowed frame view over caller-owned bytes |
| [context_alloc.c](https://codeberg.org/jimisaacs/pancake/src/branch/main/examples/c/context_alloc.c) | a C embedder owning every pancake allocation through the context's host-allocator callbacks |
| [compose_transports.c](https://codeberg.org/jimisaacs/pancake/src/branch/main/examples/c/compose_transports.c) | the à-la-carte archives composing: three headers, one link line, no symbol collisions |

## The cross-language apps

Each app is built on the same small surface of application verbs (connect,
fetch, publish, spawn, send, serve, close) written once per language, so the
app reads the same in Zig and the four [bindings]($link.page('bindings')):
Rust, Python, Racket, and TypeScript.

| App | What it is | Run it |
| --- | --- | --- |
| [rooms](https://codeberg.org/jimisaacs/pancake/src/branch/main/examples/apps/rooms/) | a capability-secure chat, real-time, with a browser UI and direct messages by introduction | `zig build demo-rooms` |
| rooms over iroh | the same chat over encrypted QUIC | `zig build demo-rooms-iroh` (needs cargo) |
| [tasks](https://codeberg.org/jimisaacs/pancake/src/branch/main/examples/apps/tasks/) | a shared task board: CRUD plus live state | `zig build demo-tasks` |
| [tools](https://codeberg.org/jimisaacs/pancake/src/branch/main/examples/apps/tools/) | a capability showcase for AI agent harnesses: delegation, attenuation, revocation | `zig build demo-tools` |
| [accountability](https://codeberg.org/jimisaacs/pancake/src/branch/main/examples/apps/accountability-on-pancake/) | a signed-call accountability app over a real OCapN/TCP session | `zig build demo-accountability` |

The `rooms` app has a browser front end, and it is the most complete way to watch
OCapN at work. `zig build rooms-web` serves it at `http://localhost:8000`: a
real-time chat with a live directory of rooms, presence, rich text, and reconnect.
It also does the move a broadcast server cannot. Pick a member and choose
_Message_, and the room introduces you through a three-party handoff; from then on
your direct messages travel straight to that person, and the room server is no
longer on the path. The conversation's own header says so. `zig build
rooms-web-iroh` runs the same page with the OCapN connections over an encrypted
[iroh]($link.page('transports/iroh')) channel instead of TCP. The browser still
reaches the server over a plaintext WebSocket, so that encryption covers the hop
between peers, not the browser's own hop to the server.

The `rooms`, `tasks`, and `tools` demos need only `deno` and the always-built Zig
client; the Python, Racket, and Rust clients join when their runtimes are present
and are skipped with a note otherwise. The `accountability` demo pairs a Zig host
with Deno and Python callers. Each binds an ephemeral port by default; pin it with
`-Dport`, or add `-Dattach=true` to join a server you already started.
`accountability` refuses `-Dattach` because each caller needs a fresh host. The
demos run as siblings of `check`, not as part of the hermetic gate.

The `rooms over iroh` variant swaps the wire out from under that same
five-language chat: the coordinator and clients now reach each other over an
encrypted [iroh]($link.page('transports/iroh')) channel instead of a local TCP
port. Each
peer is addressed by a self-authenticating node id, the bytes on the wire are
keyed QUIC, and the app code does not change; only the address does. It needs
`cargo` for the iroh sidecar.

Why an application at all? A protocol can pass every conformance scenario and still
be miserable to build on. These apps are where the surface meets a real program, in
five languages at once, and where the rough edges show up that no fixture reaches.
